# GIT & GITHUB – EASY LEARNING NOTES

## 1. What is Git?

Git is a **Version Control System (VCS)** used to track changes in our source code.

**Why do we use Git?**

- To track code changes.
- To maintain different versions of code.
- To restore previous versions.
- To collaborate with team members.
- To manage project history.

## 2. What is GitHub?

GitHub is a cloud-based platform where developers store and manage their Git repositories.

**Simple difference:**

| Git                  | GitHub                         |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Version control tool | Cloud-based hosting platform   |
| Tracks code changes  | Stores Git repositories online |
| Works locally        | Works online                   |
| Manages commits      | Enables collaboration          |

**Easy way to remember:**

- Git = Tracks changes.
- GitHub = Stores and shares code.

## 3. Important Git Concepts

### Repository (Repo)

A repository is a folder where your project files and Git history are maintained.

### Clone

Downloading an existing GitHub repository to your local computer for the first time.

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
```

### Pull

Downloading the latest changes from GitHub into your local repository.

```bash
git pull origin main
```

### Add

Selecting the files you want to include in your next commit.

```bash
git add .
```

### Commit

Saving your selected changes in the local Git history with a message.

```bash
git commit -m "Added login test cases"
```

### Push

Uploading your local commits to GitHub.

```bash
git push origin main
```

## 4. Most Important Git Commands

| Command                   | Purpose                              |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `git init`                | Initialize a Git repository          |
| `git clone`               | Download a repository                |
| `git status`              | Check modified files                 |
| `git add .`               | Stage all changes                    |
| `git commit -m "message"` | Save changes locally                 |
| `git push`                | Upload changes to GitHub             |
| `git pull`                | Download latest changes              |
| `git fetch`               | Check remote changes without merging |
| `git log`                 | View commit history                  |
| `git branch`              | View branches                        |
| `git switch main`         | Switch to main branch                |

## 5. How to Upload Your Code from VS Code to GitHub

Follow these steps whenever you make changes to your automation project.

### Step 1: Open your project in VS Code

Open your project folder in VS Code.

### Step 2: Initialize Git

```bash
git init
```

This creates a local Git repository.

### Step 3: Check file status

```bash
git status
```

This displays modified and untracked files.

### Step 4: Add files

```bash
git add .
```

This stages all changes.

### Step 5: Commit changes

```bash
git commit -m "Added Playwright automation scripts"
```

This saves your changes locally.

### Step 6: Connect to GitHub

If you already created a GitHub repository:

```bash
git remote add origin <repository-url>
```

### Step 7: Push code to GitHub

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

Your local code is now uploaded to GitHub.

**Note:** If the GitHub repository already has commits, you may need to pull and resolve any conflicts before pushing.

## 6. How to Pull Code from GitHub into VS Code

Suppose your teammate updated the automation scripts on GitHub.

You want to get those latest changes into your local VS Code project.

### Step 1: Open VS Code terminal.

### Step 2: Navigate to your project folder.

### Step 3: Execute:

```bash
git pull origin main
```

Now your local project receives the latest changes from GitHub.

## 7. Difference Between Add, Commit, Push and Pull

| Concept | Easy meaning                 |
| ------- | ---------------------------- |
| Add     | Select changes               |
| Commit  | Save changes locally         |
| Push    | Upload changes to GitHub     |
| Pull    | Download changes from GitHub |

**Remember this sequence:**

```text
Modify Code
    ↓
Git Add
    ↓
Git Commit
    ↓
Git Push
    ↓
GitHub
```

To get updates:

```text
GitHub
    ↓
Git Pull
    ↓
VS Code
```

## 8. Real-Time Example for QA Automation

Imagine you are working on a Playwright automation project.

You created:

- login.spec.js
- event.spec.js
- checkout.spec.js

After completing your test scripts, you want to upload them to GitHub.

Execute:

```bash
git status

git add .

git commit -m "Added login and event test scripts"

git push origin main
```

If your teammate updates the project:

```bash
git pull origin main
```

Your local project will get the latest changes.

## 9. How to Download Code from GitHub for the First Time

If you don't have the project locally:

### Step 1

Open the GitHub repository.

### Step 2

Click the Code button.

### Step 3

Copy the HTTPS repository URL.

### Step 4

Open VS Code terminal and execute:

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
```

### Step 5

Open the downloaded project folder in VS Code.

**Remember:**

- First time downloading = Clone.
- Getting latest changes = Pull.

## 10. Common Interview Questions

**Q1. What is Git?**

Git is a distributed version control system used to track source code changes and maintain different versions of a project.

**Q2. What is GitHub?**

GitHub is a cloud-based platform used to host Git repositories and collaborate with developers.

**Q3. What is the difference between Git Pull and Git Push?**

Git Pull downloads the latest changes from a remote repository into the local repository, whereas Git Push uploads local commits to the remote repository.

**Q4. What is Git Commit?**

Git Commit saves staged changes in the local repository with a descriptive message.

**Q5. What is Git Clone?**

Git Clone creates a local copy of an existing remote repository.

**Q6. What is Git Add?**

Git Add moves modified or new files into the staging area before committing.

**Q7. What is Git Status?**

Git Status displays the current state of the working directory and staging area.

**Q8. What is a Git Branch?**

A branch is an independent line of development that allows developers to work on features without directly changing the main branch.

**Q9. What is a Merge Conflict?**

A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically combine changes, usually because different changes affect the same part of a file. Developers must resolve the conflict manually.

## 11. Easy Memory Trick

Remember these four words:

- ADD = Select files.
- COMMIT = Save locally.
- PUSH = Send to GitHub.
- PULL = Get from GitHub.

